--- machine\system\network
canonical: Machine\System\Network

Peios Network Policy's key: what this machine's network should be, and what
netd found. netd reads the whole subtree on start and again on every
change, and reconciles the kernel to it: links, addresses and routes on a
joined interface are netd's, so an address added by hand is removed on the
next pass. The registry is the truth; there is no other place the network
is configured.

Three parts of the subtree are netd's business:

- `Rules\Interface\` — the interface layer: PNP rules whose subject is an
  interface, deciding which profile it stands in (`JOIN(profile)`),
  whether netd leaves it alone (`IGNORE`) or keeps it down (`DOWN`).
- `Profiles\` — how an interface stands on a network: what to take from
  the network's offer, and what to dictate instead.
- `Interfaces\` and `Networks\` — what netd found, written back under
  `Status` keys only netd may write, beside the few values it shares with
  the operator.

The layer, the facts, the verdicts and the profile vocabulary are
specified in the network policy reference (learn: networking, "Network
policy reference"); this file documents what netd reads and writes and
how. The packet layers (`Rules\Packet`, `RawPacket`, `Flow`) are the
kernel's and are documented there.

Nothing transient — leases, link state, what a network offered — is stored
here; ask `net status` for that. `Readiness` is the one observation kept
here, because services watch it.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and Administrators
full, Authenticated Users read). `Status` keys carry their own descriptor.

--- machine\system\network hostname
canonical: Machine\System\Network Hostname
type: REG_SZ
default: (unset)
valid: a hostname
applies: live

The static hostname. netd calls `sethostname(2)` with it on start and on
change. Absent, the hostname stays whatever it was — or, on an interface
whose profile says `Hostname.Offered`, whatever the network supplied.

--- machine\system\network readiness
canonical: Machine\System\Network Readiness
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent until netd has judged an interface)
valid: absent, link, addressed, routed
applies: n/a

The machine's readiness: `absent`, `link`, `addressed` or `routed` — the
highest among joined interfaces. Not configuration: a fact for services
and scripts to watch, and what `net wait` waits on. An ignored or down
interface contributes nothing.

Written by netd. Editing it does not change the network; the next pass
overwrites whatever was put here.

--- machine\system\network duid
canonical: Machine\System\Network Duid
type: REG_SZ
default: generated by netd on first use
valid: colon-separated hex
applies: next DHCP start

The machine's DHCPv6 identifier (RFC 8415 DUID), as colon-separated hex.
Shared: netd writes one the first time it needs it (DUID-LL from the first
hardware address seen) and never changes it; an operator may set it so a
server-side reservation matches. DHCPv4 client identifiers (RFC 4361) are
built on it, per interface — see `Interfaces\<ifid> ClientId`.

--- machine\system\network controlsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Network ControlSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: Everyone: NETWORK_QUERY; SYSTEM, Administrators: all
valid: a self-relative Peios security descriptor
applies: live

The netd control object's descriptor, self-relative. Checked against the
peer's token on every request to `/run/netd/control.sock`. Rights:
`NETWORK_QUERY` (0x1) for `net status` and `net wait`; `NETWORK_CONTROL`
(0x2) for `net renew` and `net reconcile`. Absent or malformed, the compiled
default above applies. Not PNP: this is netd's own control, and another
executor would have its own.

--- machine\system\network\rules\interface
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\Interface

The interface layer. A forest of PNP rules like the packet layers' —
the key name is attribution, `<Fact>.<Operator>` values are conditions,
`Actions` is the action list, `Priority` and `Enabled` as everywhere,
subkeys are exceptions — whose subject is an interface rather than a
packet. netd judges every interface (loopback excepted) against it
whenever an interface appears or changes, whenever a network is
identified on it, and whenever the subtree changes.

Facts: `Interface` (the kernel name), `Interface.Kind` (`wired`,
`wireless`, `loopback`, `other`), `Interface.Id` (the inventory key),
`Interface.Mac`, `Interface.Path`, `Interface.Driver`, and once a network
has been identified on the link, `Network.Id`, `Network.Name`,
`Network.Trust`, `Network.Kind`. A fact the interface lacks (a tunnel has
no `Path`) makes a condition false, never an error.

Verdicts: `JOIN(<profile path>)` — bring it up and stand in that profile;
`IGNORE` — never touch it; `DOWN` — keep it administratively down.
Strictness `DOWN` > `IGNORE` > `JOIN`; the compiled-in backstop is
`IGNORE`. `REPORT(n)` is the one effect the layer keeps. Anything else
(`PASS`, `TAG`, a `Tag.*` or `Counter.*` condition) refuses the generation.

A generation is refused as a whole — netd keeps the last good one and
`net status` says why — when a rule is malformed, a `JOIN` names a
profile that does not exist, a profile is malformed, or two rules tied on
priority name different profiles for one interface. A `JOIN` of a profile
that exists but is disabled is not a fault: that rule abstains, as if
its action were `NULL`, and its parent, another tree or the backstop
answers.

netd builds and judges this layer with `pnp-core`, the kernel's own rules
engine, so a rule means here exactly what it would mean in the packet
layers. The kernel never reads this key.

--- machine\system\network\rules\interface\<rule...> actions
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\Interface\<rule...> Actions
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent or empty — NULL, the rule abstains)
valid: JOIN(profile), IGNORE, DOWN, REPORT(n)
applies: live

What a rule does when it matches an interface — this layer's action
vocabulary, which is not the packet layers'.

- `JOIN(<profile path>)` — bring the interface up and stand it in that
  profile.
- `IGNORE` — never touch it. The compiled-in backstop, so this is also
  what an interface no rule spoke for gets.
- `DOWN` — keep it administratively down.
- `REPORT(n)` — the one effect this layer keeps, alongside a verdict.

Strictness orders them `DOWN` > `IGNORE` > `JOIN`, so the strictest verdict
among matching rules wins rather than the first or the last.

Anything the packet layers accept and this layer does not — `PASS`, `TAG`,
a `Tag.*` or `Counter.*` condition — refuses the generation rather than
being ignored. Two rules tied on priority naming different profiles
refuses it too; netd keeps the last good generation and `net status` says
which rule to look at.

--- machine\system\network\profiles
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles

One key per profile; a subkey is a derived profile. A profile has no
match block — which interfaces stand in it is the interface layer's
verdict — and its values are flat dotted names, `<Bundle>.<Setting>`,
listed in the network policy reference. Unknown names refuse the
generation.

Inheritance: `office\london` carries every value of `office` and
overrides those it names, per value name and wholesale (a list replaces
a list, never appends). A present-but-empty value means none; an absent
value means inherit. `Enabled = 0` makes a key and its subtree invisible.

Every compiled default is "believe nothing, do nothing": nothing the
network offers is taken unless a bundle's `Offered` says so, and a bare
profile only brings the link up. The shipped `default` profile (see the
`netd-default-profile` seed) is where the ordinary behaviour lives.

The vocabulary is five bundles — `Address`, `Route`, `Dns`, `Hostname`
and `Mtu` — plus `Enabled`. Each setting has its own page; ask for a
profile to see them all.

Laws of joining, whatever the profile says: netd owns every address on a
joined interface and only the routes it added itself; the kernel's own
router-advertisement handling is off everywhere (netd is the only RA
listener on Peios); IPv6 lifetimes follow the RFCs with the two-hour
floor; a manual change lasts until the next reconcile; restarting netd
changes nothing visible.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...>

One profile. The key path under `Profiles\` is its name, and a subkey is
a derived profile — `office\london` — which is why this page answers at
any depth.

Values are flat dotted names, `<Bundle>.<Setting>`, listed below. They are
matched case-insensitively, and an unrecognised name **refuses the whole
generation** rather than being ignored, exactly as an unknown condition
name does in a rule: a profile that silently did less than it says is
worse than one that visibly does not load.

Every boolean below accepts `0`/`1` as a REG_DWORD, or `on`, `yes`,
`true`, `off`, `no`, `false` as a REG_SZ, case-insensitively. Every list
accepts a REG_MULTI_SZ or a single REG_SZ as a one-item list, and a
present-but-empty value is the empty list — which is how a derived
profile overrides an inherited list with none.

Inheritance is per value name and wholesale: a list replaces a list, never
appends. An absent value inherits; a present one overrides.

A malformed value refuses the generation and names the profile and the
value, so `net status` tells you which edit to undo. netd keeps the last
good generation meanwhile, so a bad edit costs you the change rather than
the network.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> enabled
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Enabled
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 1
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Whether this profile and its subtree are visible.

`0` makes the key and everything under it invisible: a rule that names a
disabled profile abstains, as if its action were `NULL`, and the forest
answers without it rather than refusing the generation.

**A child cannot re-enable itself.** Disabling `office` disables
`office\london` whatever `london` says, because the setting is combined
down the tree rather than overridden. That is what makes it usable as an
off switch for a whole branch.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> address.offered
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Address.Offered
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Take the address the network offers: run a DHCPv4 client, and do IPv6
router discovery and autoconfiguration.

Gated by `Address.Families` — with families cut to `ipv4` this runs DHCPv4
and no router solicitation, and vice versa. This is the switch most
profiles exist to turn on; the shipped `default` profile sets it.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> address.families
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Address.Families
type: REG_MULTI_SZ or REG_SZ
default: ipv4 and ipv6
valid: ipv4, ipv6; case-insensitive
applies: live

Which address families this profile deals in at all.

It gates the whole `Address` bundle, not just what is offered: a family
left out means no DHCP or autoconfiguration for it, and `Address.Static`
entries in that family are **silently dropped** rather than refused. That
is deliberate — the family switch simply wins, and an operator who cut
IPv6 should not have to also delete every IPv6 static to make the profile
load.

A name that is neither `ipv4` nor `ipv6` refuses the generation. Note that
a present-but-empty value means neither family, which disables the
bundle entirely rather than restoring the default.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> address.static
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Address.Static
type: REG_MULTI_SZ or REG_SZ
default: (none)
valid: CIDR per element, either family
applies: live

Addresses to assign outright, as CIDR — `10.1.0.50/24`, `fd00::5/64`.

Either family, filtered by `Address.Families`. The prefix length must fit
the family (at most 32 for IPv4, 128 for IPv6); anything that is not an
address and a prefix refuses the generation.

Static and offered are not exclusive: a profile may set both, and the
interface carries the statics as well as whatever the lease brings.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> address.linklocal
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Address.LinkLocal
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Self-assign a 169.254/16 address while DHCP goes unanswered.

The address is dropped the moment a lease arrives, so this is a fallback
for a link with no server rather than a second address. Useful for a
direct cable between two machines, and for reaching an appliance that has
not been given an address yet.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> address.temporary
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Address.Temporary
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Add RFC 8981 temporary IPv6 addresses beside the stable one.

The stable address stays and is what inbound connections use; the
temporary ones rotate and are preferred for outbound, so a network cannot
follow this machine between sessions by its address alone. Costs nothing
but addresses.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> address.onexpiry
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Address.OnExpiry
type: REG_SZ
default: Drop
valid: Drop or Keep; case-insensitive
applies: live

What happens to an offered address the server stops renewing.

- `Drop` (the default) — the address goes when the lease does. Honest: the
  machine stops claiming an address it no longer holds.
- `Keep` — hold it anyway. For a network whose DHCP server is expected to
  be away longer than the lease, where losing the address costs more than
  the risk of a collision.

Anything else refuses the generation.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> route.offered
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Route.Offered
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Take the way out the network offers: the lease's gateway and its classless
static routes, and the routers' advertised default.

Separate from `Address.Offered` because taking an address from a network
and taking its routing are different amounts of trust — a profile may
accept an address on a network it will not route through.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> route.gateway
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Route.Gateway
type: REG_MULTI_SZ or REG_SZ
default: (none)
valid: one IP address per element, either family
applies: live

Pin the way out, instead of whatever the network offers.

At most one address per family is used: list both an IPv4 and an IPv6
gateway to pin both. An element that is not an IP address refuses the
generation.

A present-but-empty value is the way a derived profile says "no pinned
gateway" over an inherited one.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> route.metric
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Route.Metric
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 100 wired, 600 wireless
valid: a 32-bit number; lower wins
applies: live

How this interface's default route ranks against another's.

Lower wins, so the default has wired beating wireless — a laptop on both
sends traffic down the cable. Set it to order two interfaces of the same
kind, or to invert that preference.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> dns.offered
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Dns.Offered
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Take the name servers and search list the network offers.

One switch for all of it — the lease's servers and search list, the
routers' RDNSS and DNSSL, and a stateless DHCPv6 answer — because they are
the same claim from the same network arriving by different routes, and a
profile that trusted one but not another would be drawing a line that does
not exist.

Offered servers come **after** `Dns.Servers`, never instead of them.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> dns.servers
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Dns.Servers
type: REG_MULTI_SZ or REG_SZ
default: (none)
valid: one IP address per element, either family
applies: live

The name servers this interface contributes, in order, ahead of any the
network offered.

An element that is not an IP address refuses the generation. netd hands
the list to resolvd unchanged; how resolvd chooses between interfaces is
`Dns.Default` and `Dns.Exclusive`.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> dns.domains
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Dns.Domains
type: REG_MULTI_SZ or REG_SZ
default: (none)
valid: domain names
applies: live

The domains this interface's servers answer for.

What makes split DNS work: a name under one of these goes to this
interface's servers, whatever else the machine is attached to. An
interface with servers and no domains answers only names nobody else
claims, and only if `Dns.Default` lets it.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> dns.default
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Dns.Default
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: (absent — follows the default route)
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Whether this interface takes names no domain claims.

Absent is not `0`: absent means "follow the default route", so the
interface carrying the way out also answers the ordinary names, which is
what you want without saying anything. Setting it makes the decision
explicit and independent of routing — `1` for a resolver that should
answer everything, `0` for a VPN that should answer only its own domains.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> dns.exclusive
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Dns.Exclusive
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

While this interface is up, consult nobody else's servers.

The strong form of split DNS, for a VPN whose whole point is that queries
must not leak to the local network. It suppresses every other interface's
servers rather than merely ordering them, so use it deliberately: a
machine with an exclusive interface up resolves nothing the exclusive
servers cannot answer.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> hostname.announce
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Hostname.Announce
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Tell DHCP servers this machine's name, as option 12.

Sends `Machine\System\Network Hostname`. Off by default because the name
of a machine is information, and a network you do not control has not
earned it.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> hostname.offered
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Hostname.Offered
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Adopt the name the network supplies, when this machine has none of its
own.

Only consulted while `Machine\System\Network Hostname` is unset — a static
hostname always wins, so turning this on cannot rename a machine that was
deliberately named.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> mtu.offered
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Mtu.Offered
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: 0
valid: a boolean
applies: live

Take the packet size limit the network offers, from the lease or the
routers' advertisement.

Worth turning on where something in the path is smaller than Ethernet — a
tunnel, some DSL — and the network is willing to say so.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<path...> mtu.value
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<path...> Mtu.Value
type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ
default: (none — the link's own)
valid: 68 or more
applies: live

Pin the packet size limit, instead of whatever the network offers.

Below 68 refuses the generation: that is IPv4's minimum reassembly buffer,
and a link that cannot carry it cannot carry IP at all. IPv6 needs 1280,
which this does not enforce, so a value between the two disables IPv6 on
the link rather than being refused.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces

The inventory: one subkey per interface netd has seen, named by its
interface id — a UUID derived from bus path and MAC, so the same card in
the same slot keeps its key across boots and renames. What netd found is
under `Status`; the key itself carries the one value netd shares with the
operator. Do not configure interfaces here: write a rule, and a profile.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid> clientid
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid> ClientId
type: REG_SZ
default: generated by netd on first DHCP start
valid: colon-separated hex
applies: next DHCP start

The interface's DHCPv4 client identifier, colon-separated hex. Shared:
netd writes an RFC 4361 identifier (built on `Network Duid`) the first
time it asks on this interface; an operator may set it so a server-side
reservation matches.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status

What netd found and decided about one interface. Only netd may write
here: a hand edit is refused, not silently reverted.

Security: SYSTEM full, Everyone read — protected, so it does not inherit
from the Machine hive root and an administrator cannot widen it by
widening the parent.

The first five values are the `Interface.*` facts the interface layer
conditions on, under the same names. The rest are what the layer decided
and where it got to. None of it is configuration — to change any of it,
write a rule and a profile.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status name
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Name
type: REG_SZ
default: (always present)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The interface's kernel name — `eth0`, `wlan0`. The `Interface` fact.

Not stable across boots, which is why the inventory is keyed by interface
id instead. Condition a rule on `Interface.Path` or `Interface.Mac` if you
need to mean the same card next boot.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status kind
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Kind
type: REG_SZ
default: (always present)
valid: wired, wireless, loopback, other
applies: n/a

What sort of interface this is. The `Interface.Kind` fact.

Also what decides the default route metric when a profile does not pin
one: wireless ranks behind wired.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status mac
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Mac
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent where the interface has no hardware address)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The hardware address, colon-separated hex. The `Interface.Mac` fact.

Absent on interfaces that have none — a tunnel, some virtual devices — and
a condition on a fact the interface lacks is false, never an error.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status path
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Path
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent where the interface has no bus position)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

Where the card sits on the bus — `pci-0000:00:03.0`. The `Interface.Path`
fact.

The most durable thing about an interface: it survives a rename and a
replaced card in the same slot, which is what makes it the right fact to
write a rule against for "the onboard NIC".

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status driver
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Driver
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent where the interface has no driver)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The kernel driver bound to the interface. The `Interface.Driver` fact.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status verdict
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Verdict
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent when no rule spoke, or rules tied)
valid: JOIN, IGNORE, DOWN
applies: n/a

What the interface layer decided about this interface.

`JOIN` — bring it up and stand it in `Profile`. `IGNORE` — never touch it.
`DOWN` — keep it administratively down. Absent means the generation was
refused or two rules tied, and `net status` says which.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status rule
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Rule
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent when no verdict was reached)
valid: a rule path, or backstop
applies: n/a

Which rule produced the verdict, as its path under `Rules\Interface\`.

`backstop` means no rule spoke and the compiled-in default (`IGNORE`)
answered. This is the value to read when an interface is not doing what
you expected: it names the rule to go and look at.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status profile
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Profile
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent unless the verdict is JOIN)
valid: a profile path
applies: n/a

Which profile this interface is standing in.

Present only on a `JOIN`. Together with `Rule` it is the whole audit
trail: this rule matched, so this profile applies.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status readiness
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Readiness
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent unless joined)
valid: absent, link, addressed, routed
applies: n/a

How far this interface has got: carrier, then an address, then a way out.

The machine-wide `Machine\System\Network Readiness` is the highest of
these across joined interfaces. An ignored or down interface has none and
contributes nothing.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status network
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status Network
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent when no network is identified)
valid: a Networks subkey name
applies: n/a

The network identified on this link right now, as its key under
`Networks\`.

Set when the network is identified and removed when the carrier goes, so
it means "standing here now" rather than "was here once". The kernel reads
it to find the `Network.*` facts for every packet crossing this interface,
which is how a packet rule can say "on this network".

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid>\status lastnetwork
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid>\Status LastNetwork
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent until the link has stood on a network)
valid: a Networks subkey name
applies: n/a

The network this link last stood on, kept across carrier loss and
restarts.

Deliberately sticky where `Network` is not: it is where the next DHCP
request finds `RequestedAddress`, so the machine can ask for the address
it had last time on a link that is only just coming back up.

--- machine\system\network\networks
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks

One subkey per network the machine has stood on, named by a UUID derived
from what identified it: for a wired network the DHCP server that
answered plus the subnet it handed out, or the router that advertised plus
its first prefix. Wireless (SSID plus access point) waits for a
supplicant. Every signal is recorded under `Status` so a later proof can
replace the guess without changing the key.

A record appears the first time an interface stands on the network; the
`Network.*` facts the interface layer conditions on are read from it, so
a rule can say "on this network, this profile" — the laptop case. The
kernel reads the same record for the packet layers: an interface's
`Status Network` names it, and its `Name` and `Trust` become the
`Network.*` facts of every packet crossing that interface, so "on this
network, this door open" is the same three facts in `Rules\Flow`.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid> name
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid> Name
type: REG_SZ
default: (unset)
valid: any string; the operator's own vocabulary
applies: live

The operator's label for the network; the `Network.Name` fact.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid> trust
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid> Trust
type: REG_SZ
default: (unset)
valid: any string; no fixed vocabulary yet
applies: live

The operator's word on the network; the `Network.Trust` fact, in the
interface layer and the packet layers alike. Until the trust pass gives
it a vocabulary and evidence, it is exactly what was written, and a rule
that conditions on it is stating that the operator accepts the
identification. Editing it is a policy change: the kernel publishes a
new generation and every flow on an interface standing on this network
is re-judged on its next packet.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid> requestedaddress
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid> RequestedAddress
type: REG_SZ
default: the last leased address
valid: an IPv4 address
applies: next DHCP start

The IPv4 address to ask this network for next (RFC 2131 INIT-REBOOT).
Shared: netd writes the leased address after each lease; an operator may
set it as a soft reservation — a request, not a static address.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status

What identified this network, and what it offered. Written by netd on
every identification, so it is the evidence behind the key's name: a later
proof can replace a guess without the key changing.

Security: SYSTEM full, Everyone read — protected, so it does not inherit
from the Machine hive root and an administrator cannot widen it by
widening the parent.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status kind
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status Kind
type: REG_SZ
default: (always present)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

What sort of network this is; the `Network.Kind` fact.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status server
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status Server
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent where no DHCP server answered)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The DHCPv4 server that answered, by address.

Half of what identifies a wired network — the server plus the subnet it
handed out — which is why the key name survives a lease change but not a
move to a different network.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status gateway
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status Gateway
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent where the lease offered none)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The IPv4 gateway the lease offered.

What the network said, not what is in force: a profile pinning
`Route.Gateway` overrides it, and this still records the offer.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status router
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status Router
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent where no router advertised)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The IPv6 router that advertised, by link-local address.

The IPv6 half of identification, as `Server` is the IPv4 half: the router
plus its first prefix names a network nobody handed out a lease on.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status prefixes
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status Prefixes
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent until a prefix is seen)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The prefixes seen on this network, as CIDR — the DHCPv4 subnet first, then
each advertised IPv6 prefix.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status dnsservers
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status DnsServers
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent where none were offered)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The name servers this network offered, from the lease, RDNSS, or a
stateless DHCPv6 answer.

Recorded whether or not the profile took them: a profile without
`Dns.Offered` ignores these, and they are still written here so `net
status` can show what was on offer.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status lastseen
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status LastSeen
type: REG_SZ
default: (always present once identified)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

When this network was last stood on, as Unix epoch seconds.

A string rather than a REG_QWORD, which is worth knowing before scripting
against it.

--- machine\system\network\networks\<netid>\status lastinterface
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Networks\<netid>\Status LastInterface
type: REG_SZ
default: (always present once identified)
valid: written by netd
applies: n/a

The interface this network was last reached through, by interface id.

--- machine\system\network\dns
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Dns

Name resolution's machine-wide values: fallback servers, extra search
domains, static names, and resolvd's control object. netd does not read
it; see resolvd.regman.
